WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
Welcome back to SpaceX Daily, episode 42.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.320
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.620
Today is July 23, 2026.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.420
Let's get into today's SpaceX developments.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.440
Starship Flight 13 tonight will test

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.680
Starlink deployment from the full stack for the first time,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.020
advancing orbital refueling hardware validation.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.240
The mission marks the first time Starship

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.400
will carry operational Starlink satellites as payload.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.880
The test focuses on upper stage performance

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.960
with real customer hardware aboard,

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.180
including attitude control and thermal margins under added mass.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.360
Launch preparations include final checks

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.380
on the integrated vehicle at Starbase,

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.040
with the window opening at 5.45 pm central standard time

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.940
and closing at 7.15 pm central standard time.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.120
Sonic booms are expected over Brownsville,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.580
South Padre Island, and Port Isabel,

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.980
if the trajectory proceeds as planned.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.540
The super heavy booster will perform

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.240
its standard ascent profile before separation,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.860
while ship 39 targets a suborbital trajectory

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.860
that includes payload deployment.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.620
Weather remains the primary watch item

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.480
and additional pre-flight testing

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.240
on the booster completed yesterday.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.900
This continues the campaign's focus

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.080
on rapid iteration between flights,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.800
building directly on the suborbital tests covered yesterday.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:26.360
Post-flight data will feed directly

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.380
into the next vehicle configuration

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.560
for orbital refueling demonstrations.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.080
The recovered hardware originated

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.860
from an earlier Starship demonstration mission.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.540
Observers noted intact condition upon arrival at the brewery site.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.640
The payload serves as a public demonstration

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.400
of reentry survivability.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.820
Community members track the item

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.720
through public recovery announcements.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.040
The event highlights ongoing Starship hardware reuse experiments.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.420
NASA plans comparative ground and flight tests

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.760
of the two human landing system variants.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.220
The evaluation will assess docking interfaces,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.080
propellant transfer hardware,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:06.880
and crew transfer procedures

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.520
for both Starship and Blue Origin options.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.820
SpaceX's Starship-based lander

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.540
remains the primary architecture for Artemis III

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.560
and later missions.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.540
With Blue Origin's lander as the backup,

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.660
results will inform final certification steps

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.680
ahead of crewed lunar operations.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.680
A spent Falcon 9 upper stage from a prior mission

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.380
will impact the lunar far side next month.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.600
trajectory analysis shows the impact in the far side region

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.640
and provides an unplanned opportunity

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.160
to study crater formation from known mass and velocity.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.120
NASA and other observers plan to image the site post impact

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.400
with no active spacecraft in the projected path.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.800
Cursor router uses a request-level classifier

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.660
to route coding tasks between frontier and lower cost models.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.980
The system delivers comparable output quality

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.460
while reducing inference spend by 30% to 50% for enterprise users.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.020
Early adopters report the router integrates directly

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.320
into existing cursor workflows without code changes.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.540
SpaceX AI is evaluating multiple Texas sites

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.640
for new ground-up data centers following the XAI absorption.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.780
The expansion targets increased capacity for AI training

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:27.480
and inference workloads tied to the broader musk ecosystem

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.080
and complements existing colossus class facilities.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.140
Indigenous groups and conservationists

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.500
filed objections to a proposed South Texas land exchange

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.640
involving SpaceX facilities.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.680
The filings claim the swap would remove protected habitat

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.980
without sufficient environmental offsets.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.980
Court intervention is requested before any transfer proceeds,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.020
centering on compliance with existing federal land use rules.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.580
Observers on the ground at star-based

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.320
noted additional insulation checks on the booster

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:03.000
ahead of stacking completion after the most recent static

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:03.860
fire series.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.300
Reddit users tracked the path of a recovered starship component

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.940
delivered to a local business,

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:13.460
arriving intact with visible charring on one side.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.260
Multiple accounts reported hearing distant sonic booms

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.700
during a prior test window,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.900
confirming trajectory alignment with coastal communities.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:24.860
Launch viewers discussed expected timing

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.900
for payload deployment relative to stage separation.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.560
The sequence places satellite release after the ship

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.160
reaches its target altitude.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.920
Timing windows remain subject to real-time telemetry.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.020
Community members shared images of mechazilla tower arms

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.540
in standby configuration.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.400
The arms showed no movement during recent vehicle stacking.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.620
Observers noted the configuration matches post-catch test

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:51.240
resets.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.080
Worth keeping an eye on,

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.860
the land exchange dispute centers on compliance

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.180
with existing federal land use rules

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.140
and resolution would require either revised mitigation plans

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:05.200
or judicial clarification on the exchange terms.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.920
On the AI front, open AI raised its 230 infrastructure

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.860
spending forecast to $750 billion,

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.240
with an initial $20 billion committed to a Georgia data

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:19.280
center project,

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.800
though no direct tie to SpaceX or XAI facilities was stated.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.940
The engineering angle on flight 13 centers on how the added

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.080
star-linked mass changes the minimum impulse needed

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.940
from the Raptor vacuum engines.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:35.560
Any shortfall would appear as reduced

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.640
apogee or a longer coast phase before deployment.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:42.720
Supplying the first on-orbit data point for how payload mass

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.820
affects the ship's ability to perform the precise burns

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:48.180
required for future tanker rendezvous.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.180
Success here narrows the uncertainty on whether the current

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:55.420
vehicle configuration can support the propellant transfer

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.720
demonstrations that remain the program's next major gate.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.560
The same flight also exercises the ship's thermal

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.920
protection system under a trajectory that includes a payload

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.040
release event, adding a new variable to tile heating profiles.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.080
Earlier flights measured reentry loads on an empty bay,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.780
but the star-linked mass changes both the center of gravity

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:21.040
location and the radiative environment inside the bay

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.240
after the door opens.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.380
Engineers will compare post-flight imagery and sensor data

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:29.680
against pre-flight models to quantify how much additional

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.680
margin exists before tile redesign or active cooling becomes

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:34.400
necessary.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.600
That margin directly determines how quickly the program can move

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.520
from sub-orbital tests to repeated orbital refueling attempts.

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.480
On the ground side, the dual flow operations now running on

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.440
boosters 20 and 21 illustrate the manufacturing decision to

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.800
parallelize acceptance testing rather than serialize it.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.600
By keeping two vehicles in the test flow simultaneously,

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:02.460
the team reduces the calendar time between flights even if

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.020
individual booster throughput stays constant.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:09.440
The approach trades increased ground support hardware for higher

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:12.720
launch cadence. Exactly the tradeoff required if

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.100
Starship is to reach the flight rate needed for sustained Mars

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:19.440
architecture development. And a quick market note,

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:26.580
SPC X closes at $115.26 down 0.6%.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.740
Before we go, Starship's next data set will clarify how far

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:33.920
the current configuration can stretch before the next design

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.200
iteration. That's your SpaceX news for today.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:44.820
SPC X is trading at $115.26 down 0.6%.

